{"id": "91bfd0da-723c-4ff3-8afd-083d3e40fc29", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:38:33.835546", "source": "user", "parent_id": "40069301-c797-4142-a975-312c02c4edf5", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Initial inspection is accepted as a faithful read of the workspace. Before I authorize the main work, I need a single, focused clarification round on the two uncertainties that most change the structure of the final design. Please lock the following as defaults that will hold unless I override them, and then respond to the two disambiguations. Do NOT start writing the full final design yet, and do NOT fetch anything from the network (it is disabled; only /workspace/task.json is authoritative here). DO produce a tight 1-page `approach_lock.md` summary that records each locked default and your answer to each disambiguation, plus a short paragraph on how those choices reshape (or leave intact) your three-check outline (A/B/C). Keep it focused; the full design, analysis plan, and feasibility write-up come after I approve the approach at the next handoff. Also, since `track.md` is absent and `researcher_topics` is empty, you are working from `task.json` alone — state that explicitly in the summary so reviewers can see the evidence base. Proposed locked defaults to confirm or override (D1–D8): D1 — no live execution is required and none will be attempted (matches `submission.deliverable` and the disabled environment); D2 — `predictive performance` is read as downstream classifier performance on a held-out task with a fixed train/val/test split, NOT as raw agreement with the gold set; D3 — single primary metric is macro-F1 with paired bootstrap 95% CIs on the same test items for both M_raw and M_clean, secondary metrics accuracy and error rate; D4 — paired statistical test is McNemar for binary label schema and a paired permutation test for k>2 classes, both pre-registered; D5 — minimum conditional power requires |D_dis| ≥ 50 usable items with non-empty overlap in both the raw and the clean label streams (defer exact power calculation to the analysis-plan stage); D6 — at least two independent in-house human annotations per item with a documented tie-break rule (default tie-break: independent senior adjudicator blind to raw/clean/ChatGPT labels); D7 — ChatGPT is invoked under a frozen snapshot and a frozen prompt template, replicated to a local or open-weight LLM if no ChatGPT endpoint is reachable; D8 — gold set D_gold (human) is held out from BOTH the raw and the clean training labels to prevent leakage. Disambiguation required (Q1–Q4): Q1 (U2 — role of ChatGPT): which reading do you want me to design for, and why?  Reading R-V (validator-only): ChatGPT is used only as a secondary judge that, together with the in-house human gold, assesses which prediction (from M_raw vs M_clean) is more credible on each D_dis item. Reading R-S (source-paired): ChatGPT is used as an independent label stream producing y_gpt[i], and `disagreement between the two sets` refers to D_dis = {i : y_gpt[i] ≠ y_gold[i]}, with the headline test being whether M_clean(n_raw) outperforms M_raw(n_clean) — or some other pairing you specify — on D_dis. Q2 (U4 — scope of evidence): is the credibility claim supported ONLY by the conditional result on D_dis, or does the brief also require whole-evaluation evidence (M_clean > M_raw on the full test set) as a co-requirement? Pick one and justify in one sentence. Q3 (U3 — authority on disagreement): is human judgement unconditionally authoritative over ChatGPT on D_dis (R-A), or does the design fold humans, ChatGPT, and a senior adjudicator into a single three-way disagreement-resolution rule (R-T), possibly with disagreement-with-adjudicator kept as a sensitivity slice? Pick one and say whether the senior adjudicator is already in `in-house annotators` or constitutes a new role. Q4 (U1+U5+U6 — scope-bounding): given the workspace contains no dataset and network/external tools are unavailable, which placeholder is acceptable?  OP-A — a single well-documented noisy-label classification dataset entirely bundled by file name only (e.g., a synthetic CIFAR-style or text-classification subset you generate offline with documented noise injection), OP-B — a structured family of synthetic datasets parameterized by true-noise rate p (e.g., p ∈ {0.1, 0.3, 0.5}) used to produce curve-level evidence, or OP-C — design the protocol generically as a blueprint and leave dataset binding to the run-time. Also confirm in one line whether Docta is treated as a callable routine you can mock (D-mock), a frozen precomputed cleaned-label file you assume exists (D-file), or neither (D-abstract — design the comparison generically as raw vs cleaned without naming a specific cleaner). Return only the `approach_lock.md` content plus a one-paragraph impact note on how Q1 and Q2 change the three-check A/B/C outline.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "9beaba28-182f-4c6c-9f4d-13d5ecd5972a", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:39:22.070141", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "91bfd0da-723c-4ff3-8afd-083d3e40fc29", "action": {"command": "cat > /workspace/approach_lock.md <<'EOF'\n# approach_lock.md\n\n**Evidence base.** All locked defaults and disambiguations below derive from `/workspace/task.json` alone. `/workspace/track.md` is absent on disk (its `documents.track.md` value in task.json is a meta-note, not a path to an existing file). `researcher_topics` is empty. Network is disabled. No Docta paper, dataset, code, ChatGPT endpoint, or human-annotator pool is available in this workspace.\n\n## Locked defaults (held unless you override)\n- **D1** No live execution. Submission is design-only.\n- **D2** \"Predictive performance\" = downstream classifier performance on a held-out test split with a fixed train/val/test partition; NOT raw agreement of predictions with `D_gold`.\n- **D3** Primary metric = macro-F1 on paired per-item test predictions of `M_raw` and `M_clean`. Secondary = accuracy and error rate. Reported with paired bootstrap 95% CIs on the same test items.\n- **D4** Paired test = McNemar for binary schema; paired permutation test for k > 2 classes. Pre-registered before any inspection of the disagreement subset.\n- **D5** Conditional power requires |`D_dis`| ≥ 50 usable items in BOTH raw and clean label streams. Exact power calculation deferred to the analysis-plan stage.\n- **D6** ≥ 2 independent in-house human annotations per item. Default tie-break = blind senior adjudicator.\n- **D7** ChatGPT invoked under a frozen model snapshot and a frozen prompt template; if no endpoint is reachable, replicate the protocol with an open-weight LLM and disclose the substitution.\n- **D8** `D_gold` is disjoint from BOTH the raw and the clean training labels. No item in `D_gold` appears in any training set.\n\n## Disambiguations answered\n\n**Q1 — Reading of ChatGPT (U2): R-S (source-paired).** ChatGPT is treated as an *independent label stream* `y_gpt[i]` produced under the frozen prompt of D7. The \"two sets\" in the brief are read as ChatGPT's labels and the human-gold labels, so `D_dis = { i : y_gpt[i] ≠ y_gold[i] }`. Justification: this is the literal reading of \"disagreements occur between the two sets\" — the two validators named in the brief — and it keeps the comparison *raw vs Docta-cleaned* as the object whose credibility is tested, while ChatGPT and humans act as the disagreement-indexing and adjudication machinery respectively.\n\n**Q2 — Scope of evidence (U4): whole-evaluation PLUS conditional as a co-requirement.** A positive conditional result on `D_dis` is necessary but not sufficient; the brief's headline claim (\"improved predictive performance\") is co-supported only if `M_clean > M_raw` on the full test set under D3/D4 AND that sign persists inside `D_dis` under Q3's fold. Justification: a conditional-only finding says \"Docta helps on contested cases\" but cannot underwrite the headline claim of general predictive improvement.\n\n**Q3 — Authority on disagreement (U3): R-T (three-way fold) with sensitivity slice.** On every `D_dis` item the headline verdict is a deterministic three-way fold of (`y_gpt`, `y_human`, `y_adjudicator`). Cases where the adjudicator disagrees with both validators are kept as a sensitivity slice but do not override the adjudicator's resolution for the primary metric. The senior adjudicator is a **NEW role**, separate from the line in-house annotators of D6: line annotators produce `y_human`; the adjudicator is a senior in-house reviewer, blind to raw/clean provenance and to the ChatGPT snapshot used. Justification: R-T avoids the single-source bias that R-A would impose on precisely the contested cases the brief singles out, while the kept sensitivity slice preserves the brief's \"both validators\" signal.\n\n**Q4 — Scope-bounding (U1+U5+U6): OP-A with OP-B extension documented.** Single well-documented noisy-label classification task generated offline with declared synthetic noise injection (e.g., symmetric / asymmetric flip noise applied to a known-clean base). The full compare / clean / train / judge / fold pipeline maps end-to-end onto this one task. OP-B (noise-rate sweep `p ∈ {0.10, 0.30, 0.50}`) is documented as an extension yielding curve-level evidence if the reviewer requests it. Justification: OP-C cannot ground \"predictive performance\" to a concrete metric under D3/D4; OP-A is the minimal concrete binding that satisfies the design-only constraint without abandoning measurability, with OP-B staged as the obvious enrichment.\n\n**Docta treatment: D-mock.** Docta is treated as a callable routine mocked with documented, deterministic probabilistic transformations of the noisy labels (e.g., a per-class confusion-matrix-aware reweighting or iterative denoising step with frozen hyperparameters). Justification: real Docta access is unavailable (no installation, no endpoint), and no real labelled corpus exists in the workspace to feed it; D-abstract would leave \"raw vs clean\" undefined, and D-file requires a non-existent artefact. D-mock keeps the comparison concrete while flagging the substitution honestly. The transformations, hyperparameters, and seed are written into the design as part of the frozen protocol.\n\n## Reshaping of the A/B/C three-check outline (impact, for the next handoff)\n(A) Predictive comparison — unchanged in spirit, tightened to D2/D3/D4/D8: paired macro-F1 with bootstrap CIs on a `D_test` disjoint from `D_gold`. (B) ChatGPT's role — narrowed to a *frozen label stream* `y_gpt` under D7, used to *index* `D_dis` via Q1's R-S; ChatGPT's downstream pass/fail judgement on raw-vs-clean is demoted to a secondary reportable signal rather than the credibility verdict. (C) Human authority — expanded from a single human gold stream to a three-way fold of `y_human` + `y_adjudicator` on `D_dis` under Q3 R-T, with the senior adjudicator as a NEW role and a kept sensitivity slice on adjudicator-vs-both-validators disagreements; the conditional headline test inside `D_dis` must additionally satisfy Q2's whole-evaluation co-requirement, or be reported as null per the falsifiability clause.\nEOF\necho \"--- wrote ---\"; ls -la /workspace/approach_lock.md; echo \"--- byte count ---\"; wc -c /workspace/approach_lock.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-4bb6b5a8-08ee-4f77-abdd-d2666b441d0d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-4bb6b5a8-08ee-4f77-abdd-d2666b441d0d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Write approach_lock.md to workspace\", \"command\": \"cat > /workspace/approach_lock.md <<'EOF'\\n# approach_lock.md\\n\\n**Evidence base.** All locked defaults and disambiguations below derive from `/workspace/task.json` alone. `/workspace/track.md` is absent on disk (its `documents.track.md` value in task.json is a meta-note, not a path to an existing file). `researcher_topics` is empty. Network is disabled. No Docta paper, dataset, code, ChatGPT endpoint, or human-annotator pool is available in this workspace.\\n\\n## Locked defaults (held unless you override)\\n- **D1** No live execution. Submission is design-only.\\n- **D2** \\\"Predictive performance\\\" = downstream classifier performance on a held-out test split with a fixed train/val/test partition; NOT raw agreement of predictions with `D_gold`.\\n- **D3** Primary metric = macro-F1 on paired per-item test predictions of `M_raw` and `M_clean`. Secondary = accuracy and error rate. Reported with paired bootstrap 95% CIs on the same test items.\\n- **D4** Paired test = McNemar for binary schema; paired permutation test for k > 2 classes. Pre-registered before any inspection of the disagreement subset.\\n- **D5** Conditional power requires |`D_dis`| \\u2265 50 usable items in BOTH raw and clean label streams. Exact power calculation deferred to the analysis-plan stage.\\n- **D6** \\u2265 2 independent in-house human annotations per item. Default tie-break = blind senior adjudicator.\\n- **D7** ChatGPT invoked under a frozen model snapshot and a frozen prompt template; if no endpoint is reachable, replicate the protocol with an open-weight LLM and disclose the substitution.\\n- **D8** `D_gold` is disjoint from BOTH the raw and the clean training labels. No item in `D_gold` appears in any training set.\\n\\n## Disambiguations answered\\n\\n**Q1 \\u2014 Reading of ChatGPT (U2): R-S (source-paired).** ChatGPT is treated as an *independent label stream* `y_gpt[i]` produced under the frozen prompt of D7. The \\\"two sets\\\" in the brief are read as ChatGPT's labels and the human-gold labels, so `D_dis = { i : y_gpt[i] \\u2260 y_gold[i] }`. Justification: this is the literal reading of \\\"disagreements occur between the two sets\\\" \\u2014 the two validators named in the brief \\u2014 and it keeps the comparison *raw vs Docta-cleaned* as the object whose credibility is tested, while ChatGPT and humans act as the disagreement-indexing and adjudication machinery respectively.\\n\\n**Q2 \\u2014 Scope of evidence (U4): whole-evaluation PLUS conditional as a co-requirement.** A positive conditional result on `D_dis` is necessary but not sufficient; the brief's headline claim (\\\"improved predictive performance\\\") is co-supported only if `M_clean > M_raw` on the full test set under D3/D4 AND that sign persists inside `D_dis` under Q3's fold. Justification: a conditional-only finding says \\\"Docta helps on contested cases\\\" but cannot underwrite the headline claim of general predictive improvement.\\n\\n**Q3 \\u2014 Authority on disagreement (U3): R-T (three-way fold) with sensitivity slice.** On every `D_dis` item the headline verdict is a deterministic three-way fold of (`y_gpt`, `y_human`, `y_adjudicator`). Cases where the adjudicator disagrees with both validators are kept as a sensitivity slice but do not override the adjudicator's resolution for the primary metric. The senior adjudicator is a **NEW role**, separate from the line in-house annotators of D6: line annotators produce `y_human`; the adjudicator is a senior in-house reviewer, blind to raw/clean provenance and to the ChatGPT snapshot used. Justification: R-T avoids the single-source bias that R-A would impose on precisely the contested cases the brief singles out, while the kept sensitivity slice preserves the brief's \\\"both validators\\\" signal.\\n\\n**Q4 \\u2014 Scope-bounding (U1+U5+U6): OP-A with OP-B extension documented.** Single well-documented noisy-label classification task generated offline with declared synthetic noise injection (e.g., symmetric / asymmetric flip noise applied to a known-clean base). The full compare / clean / train / judge / fold pipeline maps end-to-end onto this one task. OP-B (noise-rate sweep `p \\u2208 {0.10, 0.30, 0.50}`) is documented as an extension yielding curve-level evidence if the reviewer requests it. Justification: OP-C cannot ground \\\"predictive performance\\\" to a concrete metric under D3/D4; OP-A is the minimal concrete binding that satisfies the design-only constraint without abandoning measurability, with OP-B staged as the obvious enrichment.\\n\\n**Docta treatment: D-mock.** Docta is treated as a callable routine mocked with documented, deterministic probabilistic transformations of the noisy labels (e.g., a per-class confusion-matrix-aware reweighting or iterative denoising step with frozen hyperparameters). Justification: real Docta access is unavailable (no installation, no endpoint), and no real labelled corpus exists in the workspace to feed it; D-abstract would leave \\\"raw vs clean\\\" undefined, and D-file requires a non-existent artefact. D-mock keeps the comparison concrete while flagging the substitution honestly. The transformations, hyperparameters, and seed are written into the design as part of the frozen protocol.\\n\\n## Reshaping of the A/B/C three-check outline (impact, for the next handoff)\\n(A) Predictive comparison \\u2014 unchanged in spirit, tightened to D2/D3/D4/D8: paired macro-F1 with bootstrap CIs on a `D_test` disjoint from `D_gold`. (B) ChatGPT's role \\u2014 narrowed to a *frozen label stream* `y_gpt` under D7, used to *index* `D_dis` via Q1's R-S; ChatGPT's downstream pass/fail judgement on raw-vs-clean is demoted to a secondary reportable signal rather than the credibility verdict. (C) Human authority \\u2014 expanded from a single human gold stream to a three-way fold of `y_human` + `y_adjudicator` on `D_dis` under Q3 R-T, with the senior adjudicator as a NEW role and a kept sensitivity slice on adjudicator-vs-both-validators disagreements; the conditional headline test inside `D_dis` must additionally satisfy Q2's whole-evaluation co-requirement, or be reported as null per the falsifiability clause.\\nEOF\\necho \\\"--- wrote ---\\\"; ls -la /workspace/approach_lock.md; echo \\\"--- byte count ---\\\"; wc -c /workspace/approach_lock.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-fa283b33-30ea-4345-94b0-193e9ef31ec8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
