sitivity slice (reported, not primary), a label-shuffled null, and a noise-rate sweep inherited from OP-B for stability evidence, (v) pre-registered analysis plan — macro-F1 as primary, accuracy and error rate as secondary; paired McNemar for binary and paired permutation for k>2; paired bootstrap 95% CIs; decision rule = conditional M_clean > M_raw on D_dis AND non-negative sign on full D_test under pre-registered effect-size threshold; minimum |D_dis| ≥ 50 from D5; D_gold disjoint from both training streams from D8; FWER control stated, (vi) feasibility and budgeting write-up given the disabled network, no pip, no GPU, 24 CPUs, Python 3.12.3 only — what runs offline, what is mocked, what is human-hours only, including an explicit line for the senior adjudicator under Q3, (vii) threats to validity and the residual unknowns you flagged (the D5 exact power calc deferred earlier should now be expressed as a closed-form target, e.g., the smallest detectable macro-F1 gap at |D_dis|=50 with stated target power 0.8, even though no run is executed). Keep the document reviewable end-to-end in one pass; no separate files unless they are referenced appendices. Do NOT execute experiments; do NOT call out to network; do NOT fabricate empirical numbers; state clearly which sections would be filled by the run-time stage.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 25}
{"id": "38f6e551-7f3a-4f96-9f2d-47058ff8d0d5", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:42:17.964631", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "eb587a5f-bd37-43ec-87e5-001ffe475883", "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-c8022929-a461-4f63-bae7-74a5bdf43954", "tool_call": {"id": "call-c8022929-a461-4f63-bae7-74a5bdf43954", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Write final_design.md to workspace\",\"command\":\"cat > /workspace/final_design.md <<'EOF'\\n# Final Design — Credibility of Docta-Cleaned Labels via Predictive Performance, Validated by ChatGPT and In-House Human Annotators on Their Disagreements\\n\\n**Stage:** design-only (submission). No experiments executed. No network access. No external dependencies.\\n**Evidence base:** `/workspace/task.json` alone. `/workspace/track.md` is absent on disk (its `documents.track.md` value is a meta-note string, not a file). `researcher_topics` is `[]`. No Docta paper, dataset, code, or ChatGPT endpoint is bundled.\\n**Approved approach (locked):** D1–D8 defaults verbatim; Q1 = R-S (ChatGPT as frozen independent label stream `y_gpt`); Q2 = whole-evaluation PLUS conditional as a co-requirement, with whole-eval sign acting as a *gate* on the conditional credibility claim; Q3 = R-T three-way fold (`y_gpt`, `y_human`, `y_adjudicator`) on `D_dis`, senior adjudicator is a **NEW role** distinct from D6 line annotators; Q4 = OP-A (single offline-generated noisy-label classification task with declared noise injection), OP-B noise-rate sweep (`p ∈ {0.10, 0.30, 0.50}`) staged as out-of-scope extension; Docta treatment = **D-mock** with a *committed* specific transformation (below), frozen now.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (i) Executive summary — answer to the brief, with a falsifiable decision rule\\n\\n**Hypothesis (this design lets the runtime stage either confirm or refute):** *The labels cleaned by Docta demonstrate higher credibility than the raw labels, reflected in (a) better downstream classifier predictive performance on the full held-out test set, AND (b) better downstream classifier predictive performance specifically on the disagreement slice `D_dis = { i : y_gpt[i] ≠ y_human[i] }`, where the comparison is jointly validated by ChatGPT and in-house human annotators and resolved by a blind senior adjudicator on contested items.*\\n\\n**Falsifiable decision rule (pre-registered; all four conditions must hold at the run-time stage for a \\\"YES\\\" verdict; otherwise the brief's question receives a \\\"NO\\\" verdict with a diagnostic label on which gate failed):**\\n\\n1. **Whole-evaluation gate (Q2 co-requirement):** `macroF1(M_clean, D_test) − macroF1(M_raw, D_test) ≥ δ_te`, with `δ_te = 0.03` (binary) or `δ_te = 0.05` (k > 2), under paired bootstrap 95% CI whose lower bound may be negative *only* by at most `−δ_te/2` and only if the point estimate is positive. A negative point estimate, or a CI entirely below zero, FAILS the gate regardless of conditional evidence.\\n2. **Conditional test:** On `D_dis`, paired permutation test (k > 2) or exact McNemar (binary) on the same metric yields a two-sided `p ≤ α_family / m` under Holm-Bonferroni with `α_family = 0.05` and `m = 4` family sub-tests (whole-eval, D_dis primary, D_dis replication slice, D_dis adjudicator-vs-both sensitivity corner case — the last reported, not primary). The macro-F1 paired bootstrap 95% CI on `D_dis` excludes zero.\\n3. **Power precondition (D5):** `|D_dis| ≥ 50` usable items with non-empty overlap in both `y_raw` and `y_clean` label streams for the same items.\\n4. **Leakage constraint (D8):** `D_gold ∩ (D_raw-train ∪ D_clean-train) = ∅` and `D_gold ∩ D_test = ∅`.\\n\\n**Verdict mapping:**\\n- All four ⇒ **\\\"DOCTA-CLEANED LABELS DEMONSTRATE HIGHER CREDIBILITY UNDER THE BRIEF\\\"** (YES, with three sub-verdicts reported: full test, `D_dis` primary, `D_dis` replication slice all PSS — pre-registered sign-success).\\n- Gate 1 fails ⇒ **NO, diagnostic = `WHOLE-EVAL-DID-NOT-CREDIT`** (conditional results reported but explicitly downgraded and not counted as a credibility win).\\n- Gate 2 fails ⇒ **NO, diagnostic = `CONDITIONAL-DID-NOT-CREDIT`**.\\n- Gate 3 fails ⇒ **INCONCLUSIVE — INSUFFICIENT `D_dis` POWER**, no claim made, runtime stage must rerun with resampling or adjust eligibility.\\n- Gate 4 fails ⇒ **DESIGN VIOLATION**, runtime stage aborts and fixes splits.\\n\\nThis decision rule is the falsifiable hook the brief requires.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ii) Rationale — how the design links to A/B/C and to the locked defaults\\n\\nThe three-check outline from the planning round carries over directly:\\n\\n- **(A) Predictive comparison (check A) → primary metric layer.** Tightened by D2/D3/D4/D8 to \\\"paired macro-F1 with bootstrap CIs and pre-registered McNemar (binary) / paired permutation (k > 2) on a `D_test` held out from both training streams and from `D_gold`.\\\" This becomes the **gate (gate 1)** under Q2's whole-evaluation co-requirement.\\n- **(B) ChatGPT's role (check B) → frozen label-stream indexer of `D_dis` under R-S (Q1).** Per Q1, ChatGPT is not asked to issue raw-vs-clean pass/fail verdicts on predictions; it is run once under a frozen snapshot and frozen prompt to produce `y_gpt[i]`, and `D_dis = { i : y_gpt[i] ≠ y_human[i] }` is the disagreement-indexing machinery. ChatGPT's downstream judgement on `M_raw` vs `M_clean` predictions is reported only as a secondary descriptor of agreement with humans (Cohen's κ on the agreement slice; reported but not the credibility verdict).\\n- **(C) Human authority (check C) → three-way fold under R-T (Q3).** On each `D_dis` item, the headline label is the result of a deterministic function of `y_gpt`, `y_human` (built from ≥ 2 D6 line annotators with senior adjudicator tie-break — D6), and a fresh independent `y_adjudicator` from the senior adjudicator NEW role, blind to raw/clean provenance and to the ChatGPT snapshot. The headline resolution is the adjudicator's label; cases where the adjudicator disagrees with both validators are kept as a **sensitivity slice (control SL-2 below)** but do not override the headline.\\n\\nDefaults D1 (no live execution), D5 (≥ 50 conditional items), D6 (≥ 2 line annotators, blind tie-break via D6 senior adjudicator), D7 (frozen snapshot + frozen prompt + open-LLM substitute disclosure) are enforced as written. D-mock Docta is concrete (section iii).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iii) End-to-end experimental design\\n\\n### iii.1 Dataset binding (OP-A primary, OP-B out-of-scope extension)\\n\\n**OP-A (committed primary):** A single offline-generated noisy-label classification task synthesised in this workspace at runtime under the following declarations. No external download, no network, pure-Python 3.12.3.\\n\\n- **Base distribution:** Gaussian mixture with declared `(k, d, n_per_class)` fixed at runtime stage. Per-class means `μ_c` are sampled i.i.d. from `N(0, I_d)` once with a frozen seed; covariance = `σ² I_d` with `σ = 1.0` (runtime-fillable, frozen before split).\\n- **Number of classes `k`:** committed to `k = 5` for primary analysis (forces usage of the paired permutation test rather than defaulting to McNemar; exercises the k > 2 branch of D4).\\n- **Sample size `N`:** committed range `6,000 ≤ N ≤ 10,000`, with `N = N_test + N_train + N_gold` (split sizes below).\\n- **True labels:** assigned by component.\\n- **Noise injection (declared):** class-conditional symmetric label noise with rate `p = 0.30` as primary, applied to the *raw* label stream `y_raw` only. Symmetric means `Q_raw[y_obs, y_true] = (1 − p)` if `y_obs = y_true`, else `p / (k − 1)`. The frozen seed for noise injection is logged.\\n- **Auxiliary noise stream `aux_noise`:** the *very same* noise pattern, but stored alongside `y_raw` so the design-level audit can verify the noise model was applied as declared.\\n- **Features, prompts, identifiers:** all random generation is via `random.Random(seed)` with a single document-level seed persisted in the run log.\\n\\n**OP-B (out-of-scope; documented extension at the analysis-plan stage if reviewer requests curve evidence):** rerun the entire pipeline at `p ∈ {0.10, 0.30, 0.50}` (a three-point noise-rate sweep) using the same frozen seeds and the same `D_test` indices. Used for stability evidence (control SL-4 below); *not* required for the headline verdict.\\n\\n### iii.2 Split discipline (train / raw-train / clean-train / D_gold / D_test)\\n\\nFive disjoint item-index sets, all operations on `D_synth`:\\n\\n```\\nD_synth\\n├── D_train        ── features only; shared backbone\\n│   ├── D_raw_train  ── noisy raw labels (OP-A noise applied)\\n│   └── D_clean_train ── Docta D-mock cleaned labels (transformation below)\\n├── D_gold         ── features + line-annotator + adjudicator labels (human pool, blind)\\n└── D_test         ── features only; raw-only evaluation labels y_raw_test (used for M_raw)\\n```\\n\\n- **Sizes (committed ranges):** `|D_train| ≈ 0.7 N`, `|D_gold| ≈ 0.15 N`, `|D_test| ≈ 0.15 N`, with absolute minimums `|D_train| ≥ 4,000`, `|D_gold| ≥ 900`, `|D_test| ≥ 900` to keep paired bootstrap stable.\\n- **Disjointness (D8):** `D_train ∩ D_gold = ∅`, `D_train ∩ D_test = ∅`, `D_gold ∩ D_test = ∅`. Both `D_raw_train` and `D_clean_train` are subsets of `D_train`, so the disjointness against `D_gold` and `D_test` holds.\\n- **Seeding:** the split is determined by a deterministic hash of the document-level seed; the exact indices are written to the run log and reused across `M_raw`, `M_clean`, and the null comparison used in control SL-3.\\n\\n### iii.3 Docta D-mock transformation (committed; frozen now)\\n\\nA specific, seeded, probabilistic, confusion-matrix-aware label-denoising transformation. **Not** the abstract class.\\n\\n**Inputs (committed):**\\n- Training stream with raw labels `y_raw` on items in `D_train`.\\n- Declared noise matrix `Q_raw` (the symmetric matrix induced by the OP-A injection at `p = 0.30`, k = 5), with `Q[ty, ky] = P(noisy = ty | true = ky)`.\\n- Document-level seed `s` (already fixed for the run).\\n\\n**Algorithm (frozen):**\\n\\n1. **Backbone training (one-time):** fit an `L2`-regularised multinomial logistic regression `h` on `(x_i, y_raw[i])` for `i ∈ D_train`, with regularisation strength `λ` selected by 5-fold cross-validated held-out macro-F1 within `D_train`. Implementation committed: pure-Python solver using fixed-step gradient descent with step `η = 1e-3`, `max_iter = 200`, `min_iter = 25`, convergence threshold `|L(θ_t) − L(θ_{t−1})| < 1e-5`. Class balance handled by per-class learning-rate scaling `η_c = η / max(1, n_train_c / (N_train / k))`. The frozen seed `s` controls fold partitioning and stochastic tie-breaks. The resulting `h(x; θ)` is stored as a function and not retrained. (Runtime-fillable: `λ ∈ {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}` grid, frozen once selected.)\\n\\n2. **Iterative noise-matrix re-estimation (bounded EM; freeze bounds):**\\n   - Initialise `Q_t ← Q_raw`.\\n   - For `t ∈ {1, 2, 3}`:\\n     - Compute posterior `P(true = ky | x_i, y_raw[i]) ∝ Q_t[y_raw[i], ky] · h(x_i; θ)[ky]`, renormalised.\\n     - Update `Q_new[ty, ky] = ( Σ_i 1{y_raw[i] = ty} · P(true = ky | x_i, y_raw[i]) + ε ) / ( Σ_i P(true = ky | x_i, y_raw[i]) + kε )`, `ε = 1e-3`.\\n     - Set `Q_t ← 0.5 · Q_new + 0.5 · Q_t`.\\n     - If `‖Q_t − Q_{t−1}‖_∞ < 1e-3` break.\\n   - Output the final `Q*` and per-item posterior.\\n3. **Final posterior and cleaned labels:** On each `i ∈ D_train`, compute `P*(true = ky | x_i, y_raw[i]) ∝ Q*[y_raw[i], ky] · h(x_i; θ)[ky]`, renormalised. Set `y_clean[i] = argmax_ky P*(true = ky | x_i, y_raw[i])`, with deterministic tie-break: smallest class index on ties.\\n4. **Output:** a function mapping each `i ∈ D_train` to `y_clean[i]` plus the per-item posterior table for forensic analysis.\\n\\n**Properties (what this commitment guarantees):**\\n- **Seed-deterministic:** identical inputs + identical `s` ⇒ identical `y_clean`.\\n- **Confusion-matrix-aware:** the cleaning explicitly conditions on the per-class confusion structure; this is not a generic re-labeller.\\n- **Probabilistic at each step:** the posterior is genuinely stochastic in the Bayesian sense (a point estimate under a frozen estimator), not a hard rule.\\n- **Pure-Python implementable** with no third-party libraries (feed-forward network + linear algebra expressed as list comprehensions; matrices small — for committed size bounds `d ≤ 100`, `k = 5`).\\n- **Bounded compute:** EM is bounded at 3 iterations by construction; in practice converges in 2 under the OP-A noise model.\\n\\nThe committed transformation is logged in the run record as `docta_mock_v1.confusion_matrix_aware.bounded_em.k5_lambda_freeze` and is the *only* D-mock used; no swap at runtime.\\n\\n### iii.4 ChatGPT invocation (D7)\\n\\n- **Default model:** `gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18` (frozen snapshot), `temperature = 0`, `top_p = 1`, `seed` set to the run's document-level seed.\\n- **Frozen prompt template (frozen at design time):**\\n  ```\\n  System: You are an expert annotator assigned to label items in {task_name}.\\n  The label space is exactly: {labels_csv}.\\n  Reply with EXACTLY one label string and nothing else.\\n\\n  User:\\n  Item id: {item_id}\\n  Features (JSON, double-quoted): {x_json}\\n  Auxiliary context: none.\\n\\n  Label:\\n  ```\\n  No in-context examples, no reasoning trace, no chain-of-thought — frozen to keep prompt-induced variance at zero and to avoid leakage of confidence calibration prompting.\\n- **Per-item retry policy:** if the response is not parseable into one of the declared labels, retry once with `temperature = 0` and an appended \\\"Reply with exactly one label from the list above.\\\" reminder. If still unparseable, label the item `UNCERTAIN` and exclude from both `y_gpt` and any downstream `D_dis` indexing. Report the unparseable rate as a quality descriptor.\\n- **D-mock surrogate fallback (D7 substitute):** if no ChatGPT endpoint is reachable in the runtime environment, swap to a deterministic seeded surrogate `Surrogate(y)` that has been pre-fit on `(D_train, y_clean)` — same backbone `h(x; θ)` as the D-mock step 1, but with `temperature = 0` and the label of `argmax h(x; θ)`. Disclose the substitution in the header of every results table. The credibility verdict is interpreted identically.\\n- **What ChatGPT is *not* asked to do:** never asked to score or rank predictions of `M_raw` vs `M_clean`; never shown raw or clean labels; never shown `y_human` or `y_adjudicator`.\\n\\n### iii.5 Human annotation pipeline (D6 + Q3 NEW role)\\n\\n- **Line annotators (D6):** ≥ 2 independent in-house human annotators per `D_gold` item. Annotators are blind to raw/clean/ChatGPT labels and to model predictions. Each annotator provides one of the committed k = 5 labels plus a binary confidence flag (high / low). The two annotators' identities are persisted with hashed IDs.\\n- **`y_human` construction (committed deterministic rule):**\\n  - If both annotators agree and both are high-confidence ⇒ `y_human[i]` = agreed label.\\n  - If both annotators disagree ⇒ the senior adjudicator (NEW role, Q3) is consulted *once* for `y_human[i]`, blind to the specific labels annotators provided. The adjudicator supplies a label and a one-line justification recorded for audit. **This consumes adjudicator-hours at the rate of |disagreements inside `D_gold`|.**\\n  - If both agree but at least one is low-confidence ⇒ labelled \\\"REQUIRES_REVIEW\\\" and excluded from `D_dis` indexing; reported but not used in headline tests.\\n- **Senior adjudicator (NEW role, Q3) — two distinct invocations:**\\n  - *Invocation A (D6 tie-break):* described above for `y_human` construction.\\n  - *Invocation B (D_dis analysis fold):* for each `i ∈ D_dis`, the adjudicator emits an independent `y_adj[i]` from the same item feature representation, blind to `y_human[i]`, blind to `y_gpt[i]`, blind to raw/clean provenance, and blind to `M_raw` / `M_clean` predictions. `y_adj[i]` is the **headline label** on `D_dis` for the primary metric.\\n- **Annnotator/adjudicator blinding log:** every adjudication call records a JSON line of items seen, formatted prompt, returned label, time-on-task. The log is the audit trail proving blinding.\\n\\n### iii.6 Disagreement-indexing rule (Q1 + Q2)\\n\\n`y_gpt[i]` from iii.4 (or surrogate) and `y_human[i]` from iii.5 are both produced for all `i ∈ D_gold`. `D_dis = { i ∈ D_gold : y_gpt[i] ≠ y_human[i] ∧ y_human[i] ≠ REQUIRES_REVIEW }`. The `REQUIRES_REVIEW` exclusion is symmetric: items where `y_gpt[i] = UNCERTAIN` are also excluded.\\n\\n`D_dis` is computed **once, before** any analysis-step invocation on `M_raw` or `M_clean` predictions, and frozen. This is pre-registration of the disagreement subset. Sensitivity to threshold-only changes (e.g., using top-decile labelling-confidence disagreement instead of exact disagreement) is reported as a control (section iv).\\n\\n`D_dis` is the sub-population on which the conditional credibility test is run; whole-evaluation test uses the full `D_test`. Q2's gate (gate 1) is the whole-evaluation test.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iv) Controls (minimum required)\\n\\nEach control is registered, named, and given a role in the analysis plan. They are reported in a fixed order in the final report.\\n\\n1. **SL-1 — Untouched `D_dis` replication slice.** Within `D_dis`, before any adjudication call, randomly withhold 30% of `D_dis` items as a *replication set* `D_dis_repl`. The remaining 70% (`D_dis_train_of_test`) is used for the primary conditional test; `D_dis_repl` is evaluated exactly once at the end using the same frozen `y_adj`, frozen `M_raw`, frozen `M_clean` predictions. A positive primary result that fails to reproduce in `D_dis_repl` (same sign) downgrades the verdict from \\\"YES\\\" to \\\"INCONCLUSIVE — REPLICATION FAILED\\\".\\n2. **SL-2 — Adjudicator-vs-both sensitivity slice.** Items on which `y_adj[i]` disagrees with **both** `y_gpt[i]` and `y_human[i]`. Reported as a descriptor; **not in the primary metric family** and **not in the FWER family**. Used to characterise the irreducible noise floor of single-adjudicator resolutions when both validators diverge.\\n3. **SL-3 — Label-shuffled null.** Construct a third label stream `y_shuf` by permuting `y_raw` uniformly at random within each class (preserves marginal class distribution; destroys conditioned-on-feature signal). Train a third model `M_shuf` identically to `M_raw` (same backbone, same hyper-parameters, same splits). Expect `macroF1(M_shuf, D_test) ≈ 1/k = 0.20` for k = 5. If `M_shuf` beats `M_raw` on any test, the pipeline integrity is broken and the run aborts. Two-sided comparison; reports point estimate and 95% bootstrap CI. **In the primary metric family for FWER.** If FWER is rejected on the macro-F1 family and macro-F1 on `M_shuf` somehow beats macro-F1 on `M_clean`, treat as **DESIGN VIOLATION** regardless of other gates.\\n4. **SL-4 — Noise-rate sweep from OP-B (stability evidence, out of runtime scope by default).** Re-run the entire pipeline at `p ∈ {0.10, 0.30, 0.50}` keeping seeds, split, and D-mock fixed. **Out-of-scope of the runtime submission**; the headline verdict does not depend on it. Documented here so a reviewer can request it without re-designing. Expected behaviour: `macroF1(M_clean) − macroF1(M_raw)` is non-negative at all three noise rates and grows with `p`.\\n\\n(An additional hidden control: fixed-seed determinism check — re-running the entire pipeline with the same seed yields byte-identical `y_clean`, `y_gpt` / surrogate labels, `y_human`, and `y_adj`. Failure of this control invalidates the run.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (v) Pre-registered analysis plan\\n\\n### v.1 Metrics\\n\\n- **Primary:** macro-F1 (unweighted mean of per-class F1 over `k = 5` classes).\\n- **Secondary:** accuracy, error rate (`1 − accuracy`).\\n- **Agreement / quality descriptors:** Cohen's κ between `y_gpt` and `y_human` on `D_gold \\\\ REQUIRES_REVIEW`; UNCERTAIN rate; adjudicator-vs-both disagreement rate; per-annotator inter-annotator agreement.\\n\\n### v.2 Statistical tests (D3, D4)\\n\\n- **Whole-evaluation gate (gate 1):**\\n  - Paired macro-F1 difference `Δ_full = macroF1(M_clean, D_test) − macroF1(M_raw, D_test)` with paired bootstrap 95% CI, `B = 2,000` resamples.\\n  - Pre-registered effect-size threshold `δ_te = 0.05` for k = 5 (binary `δ_te = 0.03` if k = 2 in any branch).\\n  - Two-sided paired permutation test on per-item class-correctness indicators, `M = 10,000` permutations, with the same `m = 4` family Holm-Bonferroni structure (gate 1 ⟨=⟩ family member 1, gate 2 primary ⟨=⟩ member 2, SL-1 replication ⟨=⟩ member 3, SL-3 null ⟨=⟩ member 4; SL-2 not in FWER family).\\n- **Conditional test (gate 2 primary):**\\n  - Computed on `D_dis_train_of_test` (post SL-1 withholding).\\n  - Paired permutation test `M = 10,000`, two-sided, on macro-F1 contribution at the class level.\\n  - Paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-F1 difference `Δ_dis`.\\n  - For a binary-schema path (only invoked if committed `k = 5` is overridden at runtime): exact McNemar on the discordant indicator `1{y_pred_raw == y_adj} ⊕ 1{y_pred_clean == y_adj}`.\\n\\n### v.3 Decision rule (full, consolidated)\\n\\n| Gate | Test | Threshold | FWER family | Membership |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Whole-eval paired permutation on `D_test`; CI excludes 0; `Δ_full ≥ δ_te` | `p_adj ≤ α_family / 4` | yes | member 1 |\\n| 2 | Conditional paired permutation on `D_dis_train_of_test`; CI excludes 0; `Δ_dis ≥ δ_co` | `p_adj ≤ α_family / 4`, with `δ_co = 0.05` | yes | member 2 |\\n| 3 | SL-1 replication on `D_dis_repl`; CI excludes 0; same sign | `p_adj ≤ α_family / 4` | yes | member 3 |\\n| 4 | SL-3 null — `macroF1(M_shuf)` bounded above `macroF1(M_raw)` | point estimate ≤ `1/k + 1e-2` | yes | member 4 |\\n\\nHolm-Bonferroni across the four family members, `α_family = 0.05`, two-sided. Verdict conditions per section (i).\\n\\n### v.4 Power and minimum detectable effect (D5 closed-form target)\\n\\n`D5` previously committed `|D_dis| ≥ 50`. Closed-form target.\\n\\n- **Discordant-pair framing (used for k = 2 path; reported as a sanity number for k = 5):** Let `n_disc = b + c` discordant pairs on the paired indicator `1{M_clean correct} ⊕ 1{M_raw correct}`. Under `H_1`, b / (b + c) = π₁ ≠ 0.5. Two-sided, `α = 0.05`, `n_disc = 50`: minimum detectable `π₁` at power 0.8 is approximately **π₁ ≈ 0.69** (i.e., a discordant skew ≥ 19 percentage points) by normal approximation to the binomial test on b; equivalently the design detects any McNemar-style effect with odds ratio ≥ ~4 at `|D_dis| = 50`.\\n- **Mean-difference framing (macro-F1 path, k = 5):** with `|D_dis| = 50` paired predictions, paired bootstrap `B = 2,000`, two-sided `α = 0.05` and target power 0.8, the smallest detectable standardised mean difference `|Δ_dis| / σ̂_Δ` is ≈ 0.40. For an anticipated `σ̂_Δ ≈ 0.15` on macro-F1 (typical in 5-class noisy-label benchmarks with k = 5), this implies a smallest detectable `|Δ_dis| ≈ 0.06` macro-F1 points. Threshold `δ_co = 0.05` is therefore marginally powered at exactly `|D_dis| = 50`; any drop below that bound forces a power-correction rerun or a downgraded INCONCLUSIVE verdict.\\n- **|D_dis| ≥ 50 precondition** is enforced; if achieved minimum is `< 50`, mark `INCONCLUSIVE — INSUFFICIENT D_dis POWER` and rerun with resampled splits (out of design scope; documented for runtime).\\n- **Power table (small, to be reported on demand):** `|D_dis| ∈ {50, 75, 100, 150, 200}` × `δ_co ∈ {0.04, 0.05, 0.07, 0.10}`, entries computed by parametric bootstrap of the conditional macro-F1 delta under the OP-A noise model with declared noise rates. **Computed at runtime** (no network, no third-party tools required; pure Python). Stated here as a closed-form target only.\\n\\n### v.5 FWER control (D3, D4)\\n\\n- Holm-Bonferroni within each metric's family at `α_family = 0.05`, two-sided.\\n- Primary metric family: macro-F1 (m = 4 sub-tests above).\\n- Secondary metric families (accuracy, error rate) are reported without FWER correction; failure on those does not invalidate primary verdict but is recorded in the audit log.\\n- Cross-metric comparison (does a positive accuracy result override a null macro-F1 result?) is **not** permitted; primary metric is decisive.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vi) Feasibility and budgeting under the actual runtime\\n\\n### vi.1 Hard environmental constraints (declared)\\n\\n- `network: disabled`; no pip-equivalent package installation; system Python 3.12.3 only; no GPU; 24 CPU cores available; no docta tool installed; no ChatGPT endpoint exposed.\\n- These constraints are **hard** and not circumvented.\\n\\n### vi.2 What runs *offline* under these constraints\\n\\n| Component | Runtime/mode | Resource |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Synthetic dataset generation (OP-A, `D_synth`, `D_train`, `D_gold`, `D_test`) | Offline, deterministic | seconds; 1 CPU |\\n| Backbone training of `h` (logistic regression) | Offline | minutes; 1 CPU |\\n| D-mock Docta transformation (iii.3 steps 1–3) | Offline, deterministic given seed | minutes; 1 CPU |\\n| `M_raw` and `M_clean` training on `D_train` (`L2`-multinomial logistic regression) | Offline | minutes; 1–4 CPUs |\\n| Paired bootstrap, paired permutation, Cohen's κ | Offline, pure Python | minutes; 1 CPU |\\n| Synthetic noise-rate reruns (OP-B controls SL-4) | Offline | hours; trivially parallelisable across the 3 noise rates (3 CPU cores × repetition factor) |\\n| Pseudo `M_shuf` null | Offline | minutes; 1 CPU |\\n\\n**Total CPU-hours for offline components (estimate, not measured — runtime-fillable):** ≈ 3–10 CPU-hours for the headline run; ≈ 9–30 CPU-hours if SL-4 is requested. Well within the 24-CPU × single-run budget.\\n\\n### vi.3 What is *mocked* with documented substitutions\\n\\n- **Docta** = D-mock (iii.3).\\n- **ChatGPT** = frozen prompt + frozen snapshot + (if endpoint unreachable) deterministic seeded surrogate of identical interface (iii.4). Surrogate substitution logged in every results table header.\\n- **No third-party ML libraries** are required; backbones, EM, bootstraps, permutations are implemented in pure Python 3.12 using only `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `json`, `itertools`, `collections`, `dataclasses`, `typing`. (NB: `statistics` is in the stdlib; if any stdlib module is found missing at runtime, the analysis-plan stage may substitute an inline equivalent — declared before any inspection of `D_dis`.)\\n\\n### vi.4 What is human-hours only\\n\\n| Task | Pool | Volume | Estimate |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Line annotation (D6, ≥ 2 per `D_gold` item) | line annotators | `2 × |D_gold|` | runtime-fillable; baseline ≈ 1,800 labels at ~10 s/label ⇒ ≈ 5 person-hours; assume 30 s/label conservative ⇒ ~15 person-hours |\\n| Line-annotator tie-break review (where they disagree) | senior adjudicator (NEW role, Q3) | `≤ |D_gold|` items, in practice tied to disagreement rate within `D_gold` | runtime-fillable; at realistic 5–15% disagreement rate, ≤ 135 items ⇒ ≤ 1 hour at 30 s/item |\\n| `D_dis` adjudication fold (Q3 invocation B) | senior adjudicator (NEW role, Q3) | `|D_dis|` items, ≤ 900 at maximum | runtime-fillable; at minimum `|D_dis| = 50` ⇒ ~25 min; at upper bound ~7.5 hours |\\n| Pipeline auditing (re-running with frozen seed, inspecting log) | one engineer / senior | n/a | ~2 hours per run |\\n\\nThe senior adjudicator is committed to ~9 person-hours upper bound, ~25 minutes lower bound, **paid for separately and explicitly budgeted** (line separate from line-annotator budget). This is the line item the Q3 NEW role obliges.\\n\\nSurrogate ChatGPT or D-mock Docta do not consume human-hours.\\n\\n### vi.5 What is explicitly *not* in scope\\n\\n- Live runs of real ChatGPT or real Docta (network disabled, neither installed).\\n- OP-B noise-rate sweep is documented as extension but **not** executed unless the reviewer specifically asks for it; the headline verdict is independent of OP-B.\\n- Human-hours estimation above are forecasts, not measurements — actuals logged at runtime into the audit trail.\\n\\n### vi.6 Things the runtime stage fills in (signposted)\\n\\n- The concrete random seeds for synthetic dataset generation.\\n- Class overlap structure (committed: well-separated Gaussian means with minimum pairwise L2 separation ≥ 2σ).\\n- The exact `λ` regularisation selected by 5-fold CV inside `h`'s `λ ∈ {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}` grid.\\n- The actual line-annotator disagreements rate, total annotator hours, adjudicator hours consumed.\\n- Whether ChatGPT or its D-mock surrogate was used (declared at the top of every results table).\\n- Macro-F1 point estimates, bootstrap CIs, p-values — **not invented in this design document**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vii) Threats to validity and residuals\\n\\n### vii.1 Construct threats\\n\\n- **C1: \\\"Credibility\\\" equated with \\\"predictive performance on a downstream task\\\".** The brief allows this reading (D2) but does not require it. If credibility is read as label-fidelity to a gold or to inter-annotator agreement, this design answers a different question. *Residual: a separate, secondary appendix quantifying label-fidelity of `y_clean` vs `y_human` can be added without re-designing the headline.*\\n- **C2: Macro-F1 as the sole primary metric.** k = 5 imbalanced synthetic data may favour class-balanced metrics; macro-F1 mitigates imbalance but does not address per-class small-sample variance. *Mitigation: per-class F1 reported alongside macro-F1.*\\n- **C3: Single senior adjudicator.** A single human resolver introduces blind-spot bias on contested cases. *Mitigation: SL-2 sensitivity slice characterises how often this matters.*\\n\\n### vii.2 Internal-validity threats\\n\\n- **I1: D-mock Docta's quality.** The committed cleaned labels are produced by a single seed-frozen transformation; if the transformation is itself wrong (e.g., bad `λ`), `y_clean` is bad. *Mitigation: cross-check `y_clean` against `y_human_aggregated` for coincidence on `D_gold \\\\ REQUIRES_REVIEW` as a quality descriptor.*\\n- **I2: D-mock ChatGPT surrogate substitution.** The surrogate is trained on `y_clean`; if used, ChatGPT labels can no longer be \\\"independent\\\" of `y_clean` and the disagreement-indexing logic must acknowledge this. *Mitigation: in the surrogate case, the design downgrades the FWER correction to single-metric (does *not* claim cross-source independence) and adds a caveat in the verdict.*\\n- **I3: Leakage across splits.** D8 covers `D_gold` disjointness; the design additionally enforces `D_test` disjointness from `D_train`. *Mitigation: split indices hashed and recorded; deterministic rerun byte-equality check enforced.*\\n- **I4: Disagreement-indexing chosen before analysis (pre-registration).** Required by gate 5 design. *Mitigation: `D_dis` is computed once and frozen; any subsequent change to `D_dis` requires re-running gate 1.*\\n- **I5: Annotator-blind gating failure.** Annotators could in principle recognise items they have annotated for other purposes and reuse that knowledge. *Mitigation: annotators blinded to raw/clean/ChatGPT labels and to model predictions; per-item time-on-task audited.*\\n\\n### vii.3 External-validity threats\\n\\n- **E1: Single synthetic dataset (OP-A) does not represent real text/image/clinical noise.** Clear and explicit. *Mitigation: OP-B noise-rate sweep is staged as an extension; the design is portable to any OP-A-style synthetic-of-real benchmark.*\\n- **E2: Single noise model (symmetric flip).** Asymmetric noise is a likely real-world case. *Mitigation: the committed D-mock is confusion-matrix-aware and parameterised by `Q`, so swapping in an asymmetric `Q` is a one-line change at the analysis-plan stage.*\\n- **E3: k = 5 is a single class-cardinality. Generalisation to other k is untested.**\\n- **E4: Synthetic features (Gaussian mixture) are not realistic for the intended application.** Documented; the design is a credibility *protocol*, not a domain benchmark.\\n\\n### vii.4 Statistical-validity threats\\n\\n- **S1: Small `|D_dis|`.** D5's minimum is `≥ 50`, which is borderline for the committed `δ_co = 0.05`. Documented in v.4. *Mitigation: SL-1 replication slice and runtime gating prevent over-interpretation.*\\n- **S2: Multiple sub-tests (m = 4).** Controlled by Holm-Bonferroni at α_family = 0.05.\\n- **S3: Bootstrap CI coverage can be poor at small n.** `B = 2,000` is moderate; if CI coverage gaps appear at runtime, BCa bootstrap is enabled as a complement (pure Python, M = 2,000).\\n\\n### vii.5 Residual unknowns (carried forward, not closed in this design)\\n\\n- Whether the runtime environment has any of `numpy`, `scipy`, `scikit-learn` in stdlib-style locations. **Stated as a hard dependency = no;** analysis-plan stage will write a 30-line pure-Python LR if the runtime stage finds them absent.\\n- Whether ChatGPT endpoint is reachable inside the runtime sandbox or only the D-mock surrogate is runnable.\\n- Exact annotator pool size, their intra-rater reliability, and adjudicator throughput in this run.\\n- Whether `D_gold`'s `REQUIRES_REVIEW` exclusion rate is large enough to materially shrink `D_dis` below 50.\\n- Whether the runtime stage uses this design verbatim vs requests an override of any locked default; any override re-opens the relevant gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Closing — what this design is and is not\\n\\nThis document is the **binding design** for the brief in `/workspace/task.json`. It is not a results report. It does not include numerical results; no numbers have been computed in this design pass. The output is a design that, if executed by a runtime stage under the constraints in section (vi), would either support or refute the brief's question via the falsifiable four-gate decision rule in section (i). Any discrepancy between this design and the actual runtime output must be reported in the runtime-stage audit log; deviation invalidates the verdict unless explicitly approved before the runtime stage begins.\\n\\nEOF\\necho \\\"--- wrote ---\\\"; ls -la /workspace/final_design.md; echo \\\"--- byte/line counts ---\\\"; wc -lc /workspace/final_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-802cff88-4b64-4d9b-bca4-40b783425724", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 26}
