{"id": "6ec8d5e4-35ba-4008-b520-c920cc440fa0", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T13:44:51.377398", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "20da6696-8d26-4394-9148-8cd204e000d3", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/final_design.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## (vii) Threats to validity and residuals\n\n### vii.1 Construct threats\n\n- **C1: \"Credibility\" equated with \"predictive performance on a downstream task\".** The brief allows this reading (D2) but does not require it. If credibility is read as label-fidelity to gold or to inter-annotator agreement, this design answers a different question. *Residual: a secondary appendix quantifying label-fidelity of `y_clean` vs aggregated `y_human` can be added without re-designing the headline.*\n- **C2: Macro-F1 as the sole primary metric.** k = 5 imbalanced synthetic data may favour class-balanced metrics; macro-F1 mitigates imbalance but does not address per-class small-sample variance. *Mitigation: per-class F1 reported alongside macro-F1.*\n- **C3: Single senior adjudicator.** A single human resolver introduces blind-spot bias on contested cases. *Mitigation: SL-2 sensitivity slice characterises how often this matters.*\n\n### vii.2 Internal-validity threats\n\n- **I1: D-mock Docta quality.** If the transformation is itself wrong (e.g., bad `λ`), `y_clean` is bad. *Mitigation: cross-check `y_clean` against aggregated `y_human` on `D_gold \\ REQUIRES_REVIEW` as a quality descriptor.*\n- **I2: D-mock ChatGPT surrogate substitution.** Surrogate trained on `y_clean`; if used, ChatGPT labels cannot be \"independent\" of `y_clean` and disagreement-indexing must acknowledge this. *Mitigation: in surrogate case, FWER correction is downgraded to single-metric (does *not* claim cross-source independence) and a caveat is added to the verdict.*\n- **I3: Leakage across splits.** D8 covers `D_gold` disjointness; design also enforces `D_test` disjointness from `D_train`. *Mitigation: split indices hashed and recorded; deterministic rerun byte-equality check enforced.*\n- **I4: Disagreement-indexing chosen before analysis.** Required by gate 5 design. *Mitigation: `D_dis` computed once and frozen; any subsequent change to `D_dis` requires re-running gate 1.*\n- **I5: Annotator-blind gating failure.** Annotators could in principle recognise items they have annotated for other purposes and reuse that knowledge. *Mitigation: annotators blinded to raw/clean/ChatGPT labels and to model predictions; per-item time-on-task audited.*\n\n### vii.3 External-validity threats\n\n- **E1: Single synthetic dataset (OP-A) does not represent real text/image/clinical noise.** Clear and explicit. *Mitigation: OP-B noise-rate sweep staged as extension; design is portable to any OP-A-style synthetic-of-real benchmark.*\n- **E2: Single noise model (symmetric flip).** Asymmetric noise is a likely real-world case. *Mitigation: committed D-mock is confusion-matrix-aware and parameterised by `Q`; swapping in an asymmetric `Q` is a one-line change at the analysis-plan stage.*\n- **E3: k = 5 is a single class-cardinality.** Generalisation to other k is untested.\n- **E4: Synthetic features (Gaussian mixture) are not realistic for the intended application.** Documented; design is a credibility *protocol*, not a domain benchmark.\n\n### vii.4 Statistical-validity threats\n\n- **S1: Small `|D_dis|`.** D5 minimum `≥ 50` borderline for `δ_co = 0.05`. *Documented in v.4; mitigated via SL-1 replication slice and runtime gating.*\n- **S2: Multiple sub-tests (m = 4).** Controlled by Holm-Bonferroni at `α_family = 0.05`.\n- **S3: Bootstrap CI coverage at small n.** `B = 2,000` is moderate; if coverage gaps appear at runtime, BCa bootstrap is enabled as a complement (pure Python, `M = 2,000`).\n\n### vii.5 Residual unknowns (carried forward, not closed in this design)\n\n- Whether the runtime environment has any of `numpy`, `scipy`, `scikit-learn` in stdlib-style locations. **Stated as a hard dependency = no;** analysis-plan stage will fall back to 30-line pure-Python LR if needed.\n- Whether a ChatGPT endpoint is reachable inside the runtime sandbox or only the D-mock surrogate is runnable.\n- Exact annotator pool size, intra-rater reliability, adjudicator throughput in this run.\n- Whether `D_gold`'s `REQUIRES_REVIEW` exclusion rate is large enough to materially shrink `|D_dis|` below 50.\n- Whether the runtime stage executes this design verbatim or requests an override of any locked default; any override re-opens the relevant gate.\n\n---\n\n## Closing — what this design is and is not\n\nThis document is the **binding design** for the brief in `/workspace/task.json`. It is not a results report; no numbers have been computed in this design pass and none are invented. The output is a design that, if executed by a runtime stage under the constraints in section (vi), would either support or refute the brief's question via the falsifiable four-gate decision rule in section (i). Any discrepancy between this design and the actual runtime output must be reported in the runtime-stage audit log; deviation invalidates the verdict unless explicitly approved before the runtime stage begins.\n\nEOF\necho \"chunk6 OK\"; wc -c /workspace/final_design.md; echo \"--- line count ---\"; wc -l /workspace/final_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-1f8de617-683d-42e2-8767-8ae073db7009", "tool_call": {"id": "call-1f8de617-683d-42e2-8767-8ae073db7009", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append section (vii) and closing as chunk 6\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/final_design.md <<'EOF'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vii) Threats to validity and residuals\\n\\n### vii.1 Construct threats\\n\\n- **C1: \\\"Credibility\\\" equated with \\\"predictive performance on a downstream task\\\".** The brief allows this reading (D2) but does not require it. If credibility is read as label-fidelity to gold or to inter-annotator agreement, this design answers a different question. *Residual: a secondary appendix quantifying label-fidelity of `y_clean` vs aggregated `y_human` can be added without re-designing the headline.*\\n- **C2: Macro-F1 as the sole primary metric.** k = 5 imbalanced synthetic data may favour class-balanced metrics; macro-F1 mitigates imbalance but does not address per-class small-sample variance. *Mitigation: per-class F1 reported alongside macro-F1.*\\n- **C3: Single senior adjudicator.** A single human resolver introduces blind-spot bias on contested cases. *Mitigation: SL-2 sensitivity slice characterises how often this matters.*\\n\\n### vii.2 Internal-validity threats\\n\\n- **I1: D-mock Docta quality.** If the transformation is itself wrong (e.g., bad `\\u03bb`), `y_clean` is bad. *Mitigation: cross-check `y_clean` against aggregated `y_human` on `D_gold \\\\ REQUIRES_REVIEW` as a quality descriptor.*\\n- **I2: D-mock ChatGPT surrogate substitution.** Surrogate trained on `y_clean`; if used, ChatGPT labels cannot be \\\"independent\\\" of `y_clean` and disagreement-indexing must acknowledge this. *Mitigation: in surrogate case, FWER correction is downgraded to single-metric (does *not* claim cross-source independence) and a caveat is added to the verdict.*\\n- **I3: Leakage across splits.** D8 covers `D_gold` disjointness; design also enforces `D_test` disjointness from `D_train`. *Mitigation: split indices hashed and recorded; deterministic rerun byte-equality check enforced.*\\n- **I4: Disagreement-indexing chosen before analysis.** Required by gate 5 design. *Mitigation: `D_dis` computed once and frozen; any subsequent change to `D_dis` requires re-running gate 1.*\\n- **I5: Annotator-blind gating failure.** Annotators could in principle recognise items they have annotated for other purposes and reuse that knowledge. *Mitigation: annotators blinded to raw/clean/ChatGPT labels and to model predictions; per-item time-on-task audited.*\\n\\n### vii.3 External-validi